  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes a therapeutic strategy map for tumors with defects in the SWI/SNF chromatin-remodeling complex, linking altered signaling, cell-cycle control, protein turnover, DNA repair, and epigenetic regulation to potential drug targets. It shows membrane-level targets such as PD-L1 and receptor tyrosine kinases, with examples of checkpoint inhibitors and RTK inhibitors, then follows downstream signaling through PI3K, CDK4/6, and Aurora A to cell-cycle progression, each paired with inhibitory drugs.\n\nA central theme is synthetic vulnerability: several stress-response pathways appear targetable when SWI/SNF function is lost. In the cytoplasm, autophagy and the proteasome are highlighted, with chloroquine and bortezomib shown as blockers. In the nucleus, DNA damage and replication stress connect to ATR and PARP inhibition, while the p53–MDM2 axis is also targeted.\n\nThe chromatin side of the diagram emphasizes antagonistic epigenetic machinery. EZH2 within PRC2 is shown acting on chromatin and inhibited by tazemetostat. Residual SWI/SNF components such as SMARCA2/4, ARID1B, and BRD9 are marked as additional dependencies, with small-molecule inhibitors or PROTACs indicated. HDAC inhibition is linked to reduced acetylation, reinforcing the broader idea that multiple drug classes may exploit SWI/SNF-deficient cancer vulnerabilities.",
